    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-fraser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-fraser.jpg" alt="Peter Fraser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-fraser">Peter Fraser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Fraser.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Office of War Information, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> became a fully independent state in law when Parliament passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster</a> Adoption Act in November 1947, under the Labour government of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-fraser">Peter Fraser</a>. The act ended the constitutional subordination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of New Zealand, which had been self-governing since 1907 but was one of the last dominions to adopt the British statute of 1931, and, with a British act passed at New Zealand's request the same year, gave Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> full power to make its own laws, including amendments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-constitution-act-1852">New Zealand Constitution Act 1852</a>. The country that took this step already had a long history: the Polynesian ancestors of the Māori had settled the islands in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> of 1840 between the British Crown and Māori chiefs had preceded British annexation and large-scale European settlement. A separate New Zealand citizenship was created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-nationality-and-new-zealand-citizenship-act-1948">British Nationality and New Zealand Citizenship Act 1948</a>, which took effect on 1 January 1949. In 1950 the National government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a> abolished the appointed upper house, the Legislative Council, with effect from 1 January 1951, leaving a single-chamber House of Representatives. Independence came gradually rather than through a break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and for decades many New Zealanders continued to regard themselves as British, with the British monarch remaining head of state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/auckland"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auckland.jpg" alt="Auckland" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auckland_skyline_-_May_2024_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">elpinto007, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Postwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> was a prosperous farming economy built on the export of wool, meat and dairy products to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which in the early 1950s took around two-thirds of its exports; the wool boom during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> briefly made it one of the richest countries per head in the world. A comprehensive welfare state, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-security-act-1938">Social Security Act 1938</a>, provided pensions, family benefits and free hospital care, and full employment lasted into the 1970s. The population, about 1.8 million in 1947, passed three million in 1973 and five million in 2020. Māori moved from rural communities to the cities on a large scale after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and from the 1960s migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a>, the Cook Islands, Tonga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> the largest Polynesian city in the world. Britain's entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1973 and the oil shocks of the 1970s ended the secure British market, forcing diversification into new products such as kiwifruit, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and into markets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, Asia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Closer Economic Relations with Australia began in 1983, and in 2008 New Zealand became the first developed country to sign a free trade agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which later became its largest trading partner. Dairy exports, tourism and international education dominate today's economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/korean-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/korean-war.jpg" alt="Korean War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chosin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo by Corporal Peter McDonald, USMC, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s defence policy after 1947 rested on alliances with larger powers. An artillery-based force, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayforce">Kayforce</a>, served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> from 1950, and in 1951 New Zealand signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS Treaty</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia-treaty-organization">South-East Asia Treaty Organization</a> in 1954. New Zealand forces fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a> and in the Indonesian–Malaysian confrontation of the 1960s, and from 1964 engineers, artillery and later infantry served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, a commitment that provoked a large protest movement; the last troops were withdrawn by the end of 1972. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-kirk">Norman Kirk</a>'s Labour government sent naval frigates in 1973 to protest against French nuclear tests at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moruroa">Mururoa Atoll</a>. Opposition to nuclear weapons became national policy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lange">David Lange</a>: in 1985 his government refused a visit by the American destroyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uss-buchanan-ddg-14">USS Buchanan</a> because the United States would neither confirm nor deny whether it carried nuclear weapons, and in 1986 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington</a> suspended its ANZUS obligations to New Zealand. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-nuclear-free-zone-disarmament-and-arms-control-act-1987">New Zealand Nuclear Free Zone, Disarmament, and Arms Control Act 1987</a> made the ban law. On 10 July 1985 French intelligence agents sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenpeace">Greenpeace</a> ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinking-of-the-rainbow-warrior">Rainbow Warrior</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> harbour, killing the photographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-pereira">Fernando Pereira</a>. Since then the small defence force has concentrated on peacekeeping, the South Pacific and coalition operations, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> from 1999 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 2001.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidney-holland.jpg" alt="Sidney Holland" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sidney_George_Holland_(1953)_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gordon Burt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and parliamentary democracy without a single written constitution; its constitutional order rests on statutes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-constitution-act-1852">Constitution Act</a> 1986 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-bill-of-rights-act-1990">New Zealand Bill of Rights Act 1990</a>, on convention and, increasingly, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a>. The monarch, represented by a Governor-General, is head of state, while the Prime Minister and Cabinet govern with the support of the House of Representatives, elected every three years. Long periods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand-national-party">National Party</a> rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland">Sidney Holland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake">Keith Holyoake</a> alternated with Labour governments under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-nash">Walter Nash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-kirk">Norman Kirk</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a> (1975–1984) combined heavy state intervention, including the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/think-big">Think Big</a>” energy projects and a wage and price freeze, with a combative style. The Fourth Labour Government elected in 1984 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lange">David Lange</a> and his finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-douglas">Roger Douglas</a> floated the dollar, removed farm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, introduced a goods and services tax and corporatised state enterprises, policies continued by National under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-bolger">Jim Bolger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-richardson">Ruth Richardson</a>. Disillusion with both parties led to referendums in 1992 and 1993 that replaced first-past-the-post voting with mixed-member proportional representation (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixed-member-proportional-representation">MMP</a>), first used in 1996 and since then producing coalition or minority governments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi-act-1975">Treaty of Waitangi Act 1975</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waitangi-tribunal">Waitangi Tribunal</a>, and from 1985 it could hear claims dating back to 1840. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jenny-shipley">Jenny Shipley</a> became the first woman prime minister in 1997, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helen-clark">Helen Clark</a> (1999–2008).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary.jpg" alt="Edmund Hillary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Edmund Hillary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Hillary,_c._1953,_autograph_removed.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photographer unidentified. Retouched by TimofKingsland., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a country of around five million people, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> has had an outsized influence in a few areas. Its nuclear-free stance, adopted against the wishes of its main ally, became a lasting part of national identity and a reference point for small states seeking an independent foreign policy, and it supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-pacific-nuclear-free-zone-treaty">South Pacific Nuclear Free Zone Treaty</a> signed at Rarotonga in 1985. The Treaty settlement process is among the most extensive attempts by a settler society to address historical injustice: major settlements included the fisheries agreement of 1992, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato-tainui">Waikato-Tainui</a> settlement of 1995 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngai-tahu">Ngāi Tahu</a> settlement of 1998, each accompanied by a Crown apology or redress. The revival of te reo Māori through kōhanga reo language nests from 1982 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maori-language-act-1987">Māori Language Act 1987</a>, which made it an official language, influenced indigenous language movements elsewhere. The economic reforms after 1984 were studied worldwide as an example of rapid liberalisation. In the Pacific, New Zealand administered Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a> until its independence in 1962, and the Cook Islands (1965) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a> (1974) became self-governing in free association with it, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokelau">Tokelau</a> remains a dependent territory. In culture and sport, the mountaineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Sir Edmund Hillary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-blacks">All Blacks</a> rugby team and the films of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-jackson">Peter Jackson</a> made the country widely known, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacinda-ardern">Jacinda Ardern</a>'s response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch-mosque-shootings">Christchurch mosque shootings</a> of 2019 drew worldwide attention.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi.jpg" alt="Treaty of Waitangi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treatyofwaitangi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Hobson, James Freeman, and James Busby (English version); Henry Williams and Edward Williams (Māori translation), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> still exists, and since 1947 it has changed from a British farming outpost into a more independent and multicultural Pacific nation. The loss of the British market after 1973 and the reforms of the 1980s ended the postwar settlement of protected industry and full employment, at the price of high unemployment in the late 1980s and early 1990s and growing inequality. Māori protest, from the land march led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whina-cooper">Whina Cooper</a> in 1975 and the occupation of Bastion Point in 1977–1978 to the opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foreshore-and-seabed-act-2004">Foreshore and Seabed Act 2004</a>, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> central to public life. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immigration-act-1987">Immigration Act 1987</a> ended the preference for British and European migrants, bringing large communities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> became one of the world's most diverse cities. The country has also faced severe shocks: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2011-christchurch-earthquake">Christchurch earthquake</a> of 22 February 2011 killed 185 people, and on 15 March 2019 a gunman killed 51 worshippers at two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> mosques, after which most semi-automatic weapons were banned within a month. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a> strict border closures kept deaths low in 2020–2021. Debates over a republic and over the meaning of the Treaty continue, notably around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-principles-bill">Treaty Principles Bill</a>, which was voted down in 2025, but New Zealand remains a stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, consistently ranked among the least corrupt countries in the world.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> shows how a settler dominion turned itself, without revolution, into an independent Pacific democracy: it broke with its main ally over nuclear weapons, pioneered the settlement of indigenous land claims under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> and carried out some of the most radical economic reforms in the developed world.</em></p>

